## THE ARCHTECTURAL ARCHITECTURE :


### User-Space Layer: The Mesa 3D Subsystem

Applications (like a video game or a web browser) never talk directly to the Linux kernel. They talk to Mesa, which implements the open graphics APIs.

• The Compiler & State Tracker:
	• Gallium3D: Nouveau relies on the Gallium3D architecture inside Mesa. It tracks the state of the application (textures, blends, viewports) and converts API commands into an internal intermediate language called NIR.
	• Codegen (The Machine Translator): Mesa includes the specific machine-code generators for different NVIDIA generations (e.g., nv50 for older cards, nvc0 for Fermi/Kepler, and the modern NVK driver for Vulkan). It compiles NIR into the exact binary instructions the NVIDIA chip requires.
• The Bridge (libdrm_nouveau):
	• Once Mesa has the compiled shader binaries and draw commands ready, it passes them to libdrm_nouveau. This lightweight helper library wraps the raw Linux system calls (ioctl), formatting the data so the kernel can ingest it.

### Kernel-Space Layer: DRM (Direct Rendering Manager)

The kernel side of Nouveau lives inside the Linux kernel source tree under drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/. It exposes standard Unix file nodes (like /dev/dri/card0) that user space can write to.
• Memory Management via GEM & TTM:
	• GEM (Graphics Execution Manager): Nouveau uses GEM to provide a clean interface for allocating and naming memory buffers (textures, vertex data) requested by Mesa.
	• TTM (Translation Table Manager): Because dedicated VRAM management is incredibly complex, Nouveau relies on the kernel's TTM subsystem. TTM acts as the "bouncer"—if VRAM fills up, TTM automatically evicts older textures out into system RAM, swapping them back when the GPU needs them.

### Execution Pipeline: How a Frame Travels Through Nouveau

When a game tells Nouveau to render a single frame, the data flows downward through these exact steps:
1. API Call: A game invokes a Vulkan draw call.
2. Mesa/NVK Translation: The User-Space NVK compiler transforms the shader into NVIDIA-compatible machine binary.
3. Pushbuf Packaging: Mesa allocates a "Graphics Buffer Object" (BO) via GEM and writes the binary code and drawing commands into a command ring buffer called a Pushbuf.
4. Kernel IOCTL: libdrm_nouveau triggers a system call (ioctl(..., DRM_IOCTL_NOUVEAU_GEM_PUSHBUF, ...)) to transition control to the kernel.
5. FIFO Ringing: The kernel driver validates the memory addresses and passes the pointer to the GPU's hardware FIFO engine via direct register writes (MMIO).
6. Hardware Execution: The GPU's internal command processor fetches the commands, executes the draw call across its parallel cores, and writes the resulting pixels to the designated Framebuffer in VRAM.

### Display, Mode-Setting & Power (KMS)

• KMS (Kernel Mode-Setting): Nouveau implements KMS (nouveau_display.c) to talk directly to the GPU's display engine. It handles monitor detection (EDID reading via I2C), sets up resolution/refresh rates, and flips the compiled framebuffers to the screen at the right millisecond to avoid tearing.
• The Reclocking Battle: A notorious real-world quirk of Nouveau is power management. On older cards, the driver manually adjusts the clock speeds (voltages). However, on modern NVIDIA cards (Maxwell and newer), the hardware requires digitally signed firmware from NVIDIA to boost clocks. Without it, Nouveau is forced to run the GPU at its slowest boot speeds, which is why open-source gaming performance historically lagged behind the proprietary driver.

### Architectural Modernization (The GSP Shift & Nova)

The landscape of the Nouveau project has fundamentally shifted due to changes in NVIDIA's hardware architecture:
• GSP (GPU System Processor): On newer GPU generations (Turing, Ampere, Ada Lovelace, Blackwell), NVIDIA offloads complex kernel driver logic—like power management, reclocking, and initialization—to an on-chip, embedded RISC-V co-processor running proprietary firmware.
• The RPC Interface: Consequently, the modern Nouveau kernel driver acts less like a low-level register tweaker and more like a messenger. It uses a message queue system to send Remote Procedure Calls (RPCs) to the GSP firmware, asking it to perform the low-level hardware orchestration.
• The Next Generation (Nova): Because GSP handles the heavily protected IP, developers are building a spiritual successor to Nouveau's kernel module called Nova. Written entirely in Rust, Nova targets modern GSP-reliant cards to maximize memory safety and stability while leaving older, legacy cards to the classic C-based Nouveau driver.


