1. The Custom User-Space Runtime API (Your "CUDA" Frontend)

Instead of relying on an established standard, you must design a minimal C/C++ API library (e.g., libmycuda.so) that applications link against.
• Memory Operations Engine:
	• Exposes user functions like myCudaMalloc(void** devPtr, size_t size) and myCudaMemcpy().
	• It acts as a memory virtualizer, translating user requests into raw memory handles before requesting allocations from the kernel driver.
• Execution Grid Planner:
	• Defines structural configurations for 3D execution grids (Blocks and Threads) similar to the <<<blocks, threads>>> syntax.
	• It packs block size, grid dimensions, and device argument arrays into serialized payload execution descriptors.

2. The Custom Pascal ISA Compiler (The "NVCC" Equivalent)

A CUDA-like toolchain requires a way to turn human-readable code into raw GPU machine instructions. Since NVIDIA's exact Pascal hardware Instruction Set Architecture (ISA) is proprietary, you must build an assembler.
• Targeting the Microarchitecture (sm_61):
	• The compiler must generate machine code optimized for Pascal's Streaming Multiprocessor (SM) layout.
	• It must implement a SIMT (Single Instruction, Multiple Threads) execution model, grouping thread execution into 32-lane hardware warps.
• Building a Custom Assembler Toolchain:
	• Option A (Frontend): Build an LLVM compiler backend that parses C++ code and emits text-based assembly representing Pascal instructions.
	• Option B (The Direct Path): Create a standalone assembler that reads a custom, human-readable intermediate assembly language you design and outputs binary machine code (.cubin equivalent).
	• Warp Management: The compiler must handle register tracking and predicate registers (conditional execution per lane) so the GP108's parallel threads don't diverge catastrophically during branches.

3. The Custom Kernel-Space Driver (The Back-End)

Your kernel module interacts directly with the physical PCIe bus mapping for the GP108. It acts entirely as a headless memory manager and scheduler.
• MMIO Space Setup: Maps the GP108's Memory-Mapped I/O (MMIO) registers into the kernel address space. This gives your driver direct access to write to hardware registers controlling the execution engines.
• GPU Memory Management Unit (MMU) Configuration:
	• Configures page tables on the GP108.
	• Translates CPU virtual addresses to actual physical VRAM locations.
	• Allocates high-speed VRAM pools for execution memory and structures system memory page arrays for Host-to-Device data copying.
• Falcon Firmware Control:
	• The Pascal Hard Limit: Pascal GPUs require cryptographically signed firmware from NVIDIA to initialize the internal secure engines and handle clock scaling.
	• Your kernel driver must extract and load these official signed firmware blobs into the GPU's internal Falcon microcontrollers during boot. Without this, the chip will stay locked in a low-power mode or refuse to process execution queues.

4. Hardware Ring Submission & Synchronization

This layer handles the execution sequence, passing your custom compiled binary directly to the GP108's internal processing queues.
• Pushbuffer Generation: The runtime library builds an array of data packets containing execution commands:
	1. Instructions to set up the execution grid size.
	2. The exact pointers to your input/output arrays in memory.
	3. The compiled binary machine code blocks.
• FIFO Engine Kickoff: The kernel driver assigns a hardware channel to the application and writes the memory address of your compiled payload directly to the GP108's hardware FIFO doorbell register.
• Fence-Driven Completion Tracking: Because the GPU runs asynchronously, the driver writes a specific marker ("Hardware Fence") to a targeted memory address. The host CPU enters a wait state, waking up via a hardware interrupt only when the GPU updates the fence, proving execution has finished.

